          "heading": "Theoretical Framework and Overview: Vector Algebra, Orthogonal Coordinate Systems, and Calculus",
          "content": "# Unit 1: Vector mathematics and field descriptions\n\nFour lessons: Vectors, fields and physical dimensions, Cartesian, cylindrical and spherical coordinates, Gradient, divergence and curl, Line, surface and volume integrals; theorems.\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of this unit, explain the physical field variables and their SI units; solve a useful analytical reference geometry; reproduce a field-level numerical calculation; identify boundary assumptions; and explain an engineering decision that would be unjustified by the ideal model alone.\n\n## Unit investigation\n\nInvestigate a 2D field map by computing a signed table at (x,y) observation points and by drawing true vector arrows. A map must record the charge signs, source positions and coordinate units. Validate the numerical implementation with an inverse-square scaling test and a reversal of all source charges. Next compute flux by summing D\u00b7n over all segments of a closed contour. A two-dimensional perimeter is not automatically the correct physical Gaussian surface for a three-dimensional point charge: state clearly whether you are solving a true 3D problem or constructing a 2D visualization. For a 3D sphere, sample normal D at equal-solid-angle directions and multiply by spherical area to recover the expected Q. Repeat with a shifted spherical surface; total flux remains the enclosed free charge as long as it encloses the source. This differentiates local-field complexity from integral conservation.\n\nThe visual check should be qualitative but principled. Near a positive point charge arrows radiate outward; near a negative source arrows point inward. A symmetric positive pair gives a cancellation point at the midpoint, although its potential is not zero there. Explain this distinction in a short written report and include one numerical location where the total vector field nearly cancels. Repeat at half resolution to establish that cancellation error decreases for a correct algorithm. Do not interpret unusually long arrows near a singular source as experimental field observations.\n\n## Guided lesson path\n\n### Step 1: Vectors, fields and physical dimensions\n\nReview the governing relationship, reproduce the numerical variant with independent unit conversions, and explicitly note one observation that would falsify the model's assumptions. See [lesson 1](../lessons/U01-L01-vectors-fields-and-physical-dimensions.html). The mathematical conclusion should follow the boundary and source assumptions, not merely the choice of familiar-looking formula.\n\n### Step 2: Cartesian, cylindrical and spherical coordinates\n\nReview the governing relationship, reproduce the numerical variant with independent unit conversions, and explicitly note one observation that would falsify the model's assumptions. See [lesson 2](../lessons/U01-L02-cartesian-cylindrical-and-spherical-coordinates.html). The mathematical conclusion should follow the boundary and source assumptions, not merely the choice of familiar-looking formula.\n\n### Step 3: Gradient, divergence and curl\n\nReview the governing relationship, reproduce the numerical variant with independent unit conversions, and explicitly note one observation that would falsify the model's assumptions. See [lesson 3](../lessons/U01-L03-gradient-divergence-and-curl.html). The mathematical conclusion should follow the boundary and source assumptions, not merely the choice of familiar-looking formula.\n\n### Step 4: Line, surface and volume integrals; theorems\n\nReview the governing relationship, reproduce the numerical variant with independent unit conversions, and explicitly note one observation that would falsify the model's assumptions. See [lesson 4](../lessons/U01-L04-line-surface-and-volume-integrals-theorems.html). The mathematical conclusion should follow the boundary and source assumptions, not merely the choice of familiar-looking formula.\n\n## Unit verification checklist\n\n- Define at least one coordinate system and positive contour or normal orientation.\n- State which source or boundary quantities are controlled and which are outputs.\n- Compare analytical and computational predictions, including one limiting case.\n- Report at least one sensitivity result rather than a single nominal value.\n- Describe which material data would be needed for genuine engineering validation.\n- Prepare a concise error budget identifying unit conversion, approximate geometry and material uncertainty.\n\n**Practice and project:** [Unit assessment](../assessments/unit-01.html) \u00b7 [Unit engineering project](../projects/unit-01.html) \u00b7 [Interactive mathematical model](../simulations/model-01.html).\n"
