          "id": "sec-1-6",
          "secNumber": "1.6",
          "heading": "Computational Simulation and Design Project: Vector Algebra, Orthogonal Coordinate Systems, and Calculus",
          "content": "# Unit 1 engineering project: Mapping a distorted electrostatic field\n\n## Problem statement and intended decision\n\nA laboratory develops a teaching fixture with two test charges separated by 0.20 m. Build a field map on a rectangular grid; first predict the symmetry axis and cancellation points analytically. Use charge settings +2 nC and -1 nC, but ensure no samples fall at point-source singularities. Compare vector arrows with equipotential estimates, and report both source positions and field units. Include a table at five nominated observation points. The strongest field on a dense mesh is mesh-dependent as a sample approaches an idealized point charge, so report a masked-radius criterion instead of a false finite maximum.\n\n## Detailed investigation plan\n\nInvestigate a 2D field map by computing a signed table at (x,y) observation points and by drawing true vector arrows. A map must record the charge signs, source positions and coordinate units. Validate the numerical implementation with an inverse-square scaling test and a reversal of all source charges. Next compute flux by summing D\u00b7n over all segments of a closed contour. A two-dimensional perimeter is not automatically the correct physical Gaussian surface for a three-dimensional point charge: state clearly whether you are solving a true 3D problem or constructing a 2D visualization. For a 3D sphere, sample normal D at equal-solid-angle directions and multiply by spherical area to recover the expected Q. Repeat with a shifted spherical surface; total flux remains the enclosed free charge as long as it encloses the source. This differentiates local-field complexity from integral conservation.\n\nThe visual check should be qualitative but principled. Near a positive point charge arrows radiate outward; near a negative source arrows point inward. A symmetric positive pair gives a cancellation point at the midpoint, although its potential is not zero there. Explain this distinction in a short written report and include one numerical location where the total vector field nearly cancels. Repeat at half resolution to establish that cancellation error decreases for a correct algorithm. Do not interpret unusually long arrows near a singular source as experimental field observations.\n\n## Submission requirements\n\nFollow the seven evidence deliverables and the 100-point project rubric in the [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html). The unit-specific source model, investigation plan, parameter choices and physical limitations above determine whether the report is adequate.\n\n## Submission safeguard\n\nAll activities are **simulation and analysis only**. If a project might ordinarily be performed with mains current, charged capacitors, electromagnets, high-voltage insulation or RF power, replace hardware with the ideal mathematical model and a synthetic dataset. Do not treat this project brief as authorization or guidance to perform dangerous laboratory measurements.\n"
