          "heading": "Computational Simulation and Design Project: Electrostatic Fields, Coulomb Force, and Field Intensity",
          "content": "# Unit 2 engineering project: Gaussian symmetry and shielding decision\n\n## Problem statement and intended decision\n\nA sensor body is approximated by a spherical shell with a concentric charge distribution. Calculate radial E in the empty interior and outside, then change the enclosed charge by a known increment. Contrast a full closed metal enclosure with one containing a measurement opening. Provide an explicit list of idealizations: electrostatics, smooth surface, perfect conductor, isolated source and no unmodeled grounding path. The project decision is whether Gauss\u2019s law alone determines a local field after symmetry is broken. Explain why the law remains valid even when it no longer solves for E directly.\n\n## Detailed investigation plan\n\nUse a nonuniform spherical charge density as a test of the difference between enclosed charge and total charge. For an ideal distribution rho_v(r)=rho0(r/R) inside radius R, integrate 4\u03c0r\u00b2rho_v(r) dr to obtain Qenclosed(r)=\u03c0rho0 r\u2074/R. The radial field then scales as r\u00b2 inside. Compare this result with the linear-in-r field for uniform volume charge. Outside the charged sphere both fields collapse to the same inverse-square expression when the total charges are matched. This is a more convincing use of Gauss\u2019s law than memorizing a few geometries.\n\nAdd a conductor-shell scenario. With the field inside metal zero in electrostatic equilibrium, determine what induced surface charges are needed if the cavity contains an isolated point charge. Use conservation of charge on the shell to find charge on its outer surface when the shell began neutral. Finally discuss why an insulating shell does not support the same redistribution and why a finite opening changes perfect shielding. Keep \u201cfree charge,\u201d \u201cenclosed charge\u201d and \u201cnet total charge\u201d as separate labels throughout.\n\n## Submission requirements\n\nFollow the seven evidence deliverables and the 100-point project rubric in the [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html). The unit-specific source model, investigation plan, parameter choices and physical limitations above determine whether the report is adequate.\n\n## Submission safeguard\n\nAll activities are **simulation and analysis only**. If a project might ordinarily be performed with mains current, charged capacitors, electromagnets, high-voltage insulation or RF power, replace hardware with the ideal mathematical model and a synthetic dataset. Do not treat this project brief as authorization or guidance to perform dangerous laboratory measurements.\n"
