          "heading": "Theoretical Framework and Overview: Gauss Law, Electric Flux Density, and Divergence",
          "content": "# Unit 3: Electric potential and boundary-value problems\n\nFour lessons: Potential, work and path independence, Poisson\u2019s and Laplace\u2019s equations, Uniqueness and boundary conditions, Finite-difference electrostatics and mesh checks.\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of this unit, explain the physical field variables and their SI units; solve a useful analytical reference geometry; reproduce a field-level numerical calculation; identify boundary assumptions; and explain an engineering decision that would be unjustified by the ideal model alone.\n\n## Unit investigation\n\nImplement a 2D five-point-stencil potential solver, starting with a region with fixed left and right electrode potentials and insulated top and bottom boundaries. Because top and bottom are Neumann boundaries, carefully mirror or difference the normal derivative so it vanishes; do not silently clamp them to zero volts. Compare with the 1D analytical linear ramp. Define a residual R_ij=4V_ij-(V_{i+1,j}+V_{i-1,j}+V_{i,j+1}+V_{i,j-1}). Show both the maximum residual and max deviation from the benchmark. Refining the grid should not change the exact ideal ramp beyond roundoff and incomplete iteration.\n\nIntroduce a dielectric interface with a different \u03b5r on one half. The stencil must change to preserve normal D continuity; using simple unweighted arithmetic averages across the interface is incorrect. Derive the face-flux balance using conductance-like coefficients epsilon_face/h\u00b2. Compare grid solutions with a hand-calculated one-dimensional layered slab. Plot normal E on both sides of the interface; its discontinuity is physical while Dn remains continuous without free interfacial charge. Report grid dimensions, dielectric assumptions, stopping tolerance and an energy estimate to enable reproducibility.\n\n## Guided lesson path\n\n### Step 1: Potential, work and path independence\n\nReview the governing relationship, reproduce the numerical variant with independent unit conversions, and explicitly note one observation that would falsify the model's assumptions. See [lesson 1](../lessons/U03-L01-potential-work-and-path-independence.html). The mathematical conclusion should follow the boundary and source assumptions, not merely the choice of familiar-looking formula.\n\n### Step 2: Poisson\u2019s and Laplace\u2019s equations\n\nReview the governing relationship, reproduce the numerical variant with independent unit conversions, and explicitly note one observation that would falsify the model's assumptions. See [lesson 2](../lessons/U03-L02-poissons-and-laplaces-equations.html). The mathematical conclusion should follow the boundary and source assumptions, not merely the choice of familiar-looking formula.\n\n### Step 3: Uniqueness and boundary conditions\n\nReview the governing relationship, reproduce the numerical variant with independent unit conversions, and explicitly note one observation that would falsify the model's assumptions. See [lesson 3](../lessons/U03-L03-uniqueness-and-boundary-conditions.html). The mathematical conclusion should follow the boundary and source assumptions, not merely the choice of familiar-looking formula.\n\n### Step 4: Finite-difference electrostatics and mesh checks\n\nReview the governing relationship, reproduce the numerical variant with independent unit conversions, and explicitly note one observation that would falsify the model's assumptions. See [lesson 4](../lessons/U03-L04-finite-difference-electrostatics-and-mesh-checks.html). The mathematical conclusion should follow the boundary and source assumptions, not merely the choice of familiar-looking formula.\n\n## Unit verification checklist\n\n- Define at least one coordinate system and positive contour or normal orientation.\n- State which source or boundary quantities are controlled and which are outputs.\n- Compare analytical and computational predictions, including one limiting case.\n- Report at least one sensitivity result rather than a single nominal value.\n- Describe which material data would be needed for genuine engineering validation.\n- Prepare a concise error budget identifying unit conversion, approximate geometry and material uncertainty.\n\n**Practice and project:** [Unit assessment](../assessments/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Unit engineering project](../projects/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Interactive mathematical model](../simulations/model-03.html).\n"
