        {
          "id": "sec-3-2",
          "secNumber": "3.2",
          "heading": "Potential, work and path independence",
          "content": "# Unit 3, lesson 1 \u2014 Potential, work and path independence\n\n**Book 21 \u2014 Electromagnetic Field Theory I** \u00b7 Prerequisites: multivariable calculus, introductory circuits and SI units.  \n**Quality note:** Original educational draft; physical equations and instructional solutions require independent review before publication.\n\n## Physical idea and meaning\n\nElectric potential translates a conservative electric field into energy per unit charge. The voltage difference between two points equals minus the line integral of E along a path. In electrostatics the result is path-independent, so a scalar potential is defined up to an arbitrary constant. Engineers set a useful reference such as V=0 at infinity for localized charge or at a selected ground electrode. Ground is a boundary condition, not a physical sink for an unlimited amount of charge.\n\n## Governing relationships and derivation pathway\n\nV(B)-V(A) = -\u222bA\u2192B E\u00b7dl. For a point source V(r)=Q/(4\u03c0epsilon0 r) when infinity is the reference, and E=-grad V. Work done by the electric field on charge q is q[V(A)-V(B)].\n\n\n## Worked numerical illustration\n\nBetween ideal plates 10 mm apart with V(top)=100 V and V(bottom)=0 V, ignoring fringing, |E|=100/0.010=10,000 V/m. The field points from the high-potential plate toward the low-potential plate.\n\n### Independent numerical variant\n\nAcross ideal plates 40 V apart by 4 mm, E magnitude is 10 kV/m. Moving +1 nC down the potential by 40 V releases field work 40 nJ.\n\n\n## Engineering context: Measuring work from voltages\n\nA voltmeter reports a difference, not a locally unique absolute potential. Choose a reference node and measure two points along a nominally uniform parallel-plate region. The estimated electric field is the negative change of voltage divided by distance. Repeat the test with reversed probes; the display sign reverses but the physical field does not. Near the plate edge, equipotential contours bend and the simple voltage-spacing model should be explicitly labeled an approximation.\n\n## Sensitivity and design interpretation\n\nDoubling parallel-plate separation at fixed voltage halves E, whereas doubling voltage at fixed separation doubles E.\n\n\n## Why the model can fail\n\nA negative test charge moves toward higher potential if it follows the electrostatic force. Potential increases and decreases should not be confused with potential energy, which is U=qV and changes sign with q.\n\n\n## Two resolved conceptual checkpoints\n\n**Question A.** How does E relate to the spacing of equipotential contours?\n\n**Reasoned answer.** Where nearby equipotentials differ by the same voltage across a shorter distance, \n\n**Question B.** Does choosing a different ground reference alter force between charges?\n\n**Reasoned answer.** No. Adding a constant to V leaves grad V unchanged, so E and electrostatic force are unaffected.\n\n## Next steps\n\n[Unit 3 interactive model](../simulations/model-03.html) \u00b7 [Assessment](../assessments/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Project](../projects/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html).\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-3-3",
          "secNumber": "3.3",
          "heading": "Poisson\u2019s and Laplace\u2019s equations",
          "content": "# Unit 3, lesson 2 \u2014 Poisson\u2019s and Laplace\u2019s equations\n\n**Book 21 \u2014 Electromagnetic Field Theory I** \u00b7 Prerequisites: multivariable calculus, introductory circuits and SI units.  \n**Quality note:** Original educational draft; physical equations and instructional solutions require independent review before publication.\n\n## Physical idea and meaning\n\nCombining Gauss\u2019s law with E=-grad V gives an equation for potential. Poisson\u2019s equation includes local charge density; Laplace\u2019s equation applies where charge density vanishes in a homogeneous medium. These are not merely algebra exercises: electrode geometry is encoded through boundary conditions, and the resulting potential predicts field concentrations that govern dielectric failure, sensor sensitivity and capacitance.\n\n## Governing relationships and derivation pathway\n\nIn a uniform isotropic dielectric, div(epsilon grad V)=-rho_free; if epsilon is constant, \u2207\u00b2V=-rho_free/epsilon. In charge-free homogeneous material \u2207\u00b2V=0. Cartesian Laplacian is \u2202\u00b2V/\u2202x\u00b2+\u2202\u00b2V/\u2202y\u00b2+\u2202\u00b2V/\u2202z\u00b2.\n\n\n## Worked numerical illustration\n\nFor one-dimensional plate geometry with no space charge, d\u00b2V/dx\u00b2=0. Imposing V(0)=0 and V(d)=V0 gives V(x)=V0 x/d and uniform E_x=-V0/d. This simple derivation is the reference against which finite-difference plate solutions should be tested.\n\n### Independent numerical variant\n\nIn a charge-free 1D region with V(0)=10 V and V(.02)=30 V, V(x)=10+1000x volts and E=-1000 V/m throughout.\n\n\n## Engineering context: Numerical Laplacian benchmark\n\nA finite-difference solver can reproduce a straight voltage ramp between ideal infinite electrodes without spatial charge. This is the first correctness benchmark; only after passing it should the solver handle more complicated shapes. Compare mean-square residual with maximum field error and report both. A model with potential nearly correct at every node may still give inaccurate E near a singular boundary, because differentiation amplifies grid-level errors. That distinction is essential when predicting dielectric stress.\n\n## Sensitivity and design interpretation\n\nDoubling all lengths while preserving electrode voltages approximately halves E for geometrically similar charge-free Laplace solutions.\n\n\n## Why the model can fail\n\nLaplace\u2019s equation does not say potential is constant; linear and curved harmonic solutions exist. At an interface between unequal permittivities, one must apply boundary conditions rather than continue the same single-material equation blindly.\n\n\n## Two resolved conceptual checkpoints\n\n**Question A.** Why does no volume free charge produce Laplace rather than Poisson?\n\n**Reasoned answer.** With rho_free=0 in a homogeneous linear dielectric, the source term of Poisson vanishes. Boundary voltages still generate nonzero V and E.\n\n**Question B.** What mistake results from applying an equal-spacing five-point stencil across sharply different \u03b5?\n\n**Reasoned answer.** It ignores flux continuity; a conservative face-weighted discretization is required at material interfaces.\n\n## Next steps\n\n[Unit 3 interactive model](../simulations/model-03.html) \u00b7 [Assessment](../assessments/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Project](../projects/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html).\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-3-4",
          "secNumber": "3.4",
          "heading": "Uniqueness and boundary conditions",
          "content": "# Unit 3, lesson 3 \u2014 Uniqueness and boundary conditions\n\n**Book 21 \u2014 Electromagnetic Field Theory I** \u00b7 Prerequisites: multivariable calculus, introductory circuits and SI units.  \n**Quality note:** Original educational draft; physical equations and instructional solutions require independent review before publication.\n\n## Physical idea and meaning\n\nA field solution is useful only if its sources and boundaries are specified. Dirichlet conditions prescribe potential; Neumann conditions prescribe normal potential derivative or displacement flux. Mixed conditions are common on real structures. For a well-posed electrostatic problem, the uniqueness theorem justifies comparison between a guessed analytical solution and a numerical solver: if they satisfy the governing equation and the complete boundary data, they represent the same physical solution.\n\n## Governing relationships and derivation pathway\n\nDirichlet: V|boundary = known function. Neumann: -epsilon \u2202V/\u2202n = specified Dn. Interface conditions: tangential E continuous in electrostatics; jump in Dn equals free surface charge. Pure Neumann problems generally determine V only up to an additive constant and require compatibility of total flux with enclosed free charge.\n\n\n## Worked numerical illustration\n\nConsider a metal enclosure set to 0 V with a central electrode at 10 V. Changing only the outer wall to 5 V shifts field differences if the inner remains at 10 V; raising both by 5 V leaves E unchanged. This isolates absolute-potential references from actual voltage differences.\n\n### Independent numerical variant\n\nIf both electrodes shift from (0,20) V to (100,120) V, E remains unchanged. If only the high electrode shifts to 120 V, E changes fivefold relative to the original 20 V difference.\n\n\n## Engineering context: Floating conductors\n\nImagine a metal insert inside an insulating enclosure. If the insert is insulated from external ground, its potential cannot simply be fixed to zero. Instead, solve an unknown constant potential that also satisfies its net-charge condition. The distinction affects capacitance measurements and capacitive sensor calibration. When reporting results, give conductor identities, prescribed potentials, floating-charge constraints and permittivities. Two images of the same geometry can represent different boundary-value problems if those labels differ.\n\n## Sensitivity and design interpretation\n\nRaising both prescribed electrode voltages by the same constant leaves E unchanged, but changing only one boundary changes the field.\n\n\n## Why the model can fail\n\nA floating conductor cannot be modeled automatically as a prescribed zero-voltage conductor; its net charge and unknown potential must be solved together. Numerical instability near corners may come from boundary idealization, not a programming bug.\n\n\n## Two resolved conceptual checkpoints\n\n**Question A.** What extra constraint is required for a floating conductor?\n\n**Reasoned answer.** Its unknown constant potential must be solved together with the total charge condition. Setting it to ground changes the physical problem.\n\n**Question B.** What information does a pure-Neumann formulation fail to determine?\n\n**Reasoned answer.** An arbitrary additive constant of potential remains; charge compatibility and a reference convention are needed.\n\n## Next steps\n\n[Unit 3 interactive model](../simulations/model-03.html) \u00b7 [Assessment](../assessments/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Project](../projects/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html).\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-3-5",
          "secNumber": "3.5",
          "heading": "Finite-difference electrostatics and mesh checks",
          "content": "# Unit 3, lesson 4 \u2014 Finite-difference electrostatics and mesh checks\n\n**Book 21 \u2014 Electromagnetic Field Theory I** \u00b7 Prerequisites: multivariable calculus, introductory circuits and SI units.  \n**Quality note:** Original educational draft; physical equations and instructional solutions require independent review before publication.\n\n## Physical idea and meaning\n\nFor complex cross sections, replace derivatives by finite differences on a grid. At an interior uniform-grid node in a two-dimensional charge-free region, the potential equals the average of its four nearest neighbors. Successive over-relaxation can accelerate convergence, but a small residual is not proof that the geometry is resolved. Mesh refinement and a known analytical benchmark are needed to separate iteration error from discretization error.\n\n## Governing relationships and derivation pathway\n\nWith grid spacing h and homogeneous epsilon, V(i,j)\u2248[V(i+1,j)+V(i-1,j)+V(i,j+1)+V(i,j-1)]/4. For nonuniform epsilon use a conservative face-flux discretization of div(epsilon grad V), not a na\u00efve constant-epsilon stencil.\n\n\n## Worked numerical illustration\n\nOn a 1D plate with 100 V across 5 equal steps, exact interior potentials are 20,40,60,80 V. A solver returning 19.9,40.1,60.0,79.8 V may have converged poorly or used inconsistent boundaries; report max absolute deviation.\n\n### Independent numerical variant\n\nA 1D plate split into ten equal 1 mm cells with 100 V across it has node potentials 0,10,...,100 V. The residual of the constant-step second difference is zero in exact arithmetic.\n\n\n## Engineering context: Residual versus convergence\n\nAt each relaxation iteration, evaluate the local discrete Laplacian residual and track its maximum. Also compare successive meshes with h, h/2 and h/4 to estimate resolution error. Use the plate benchmark, an analytical concentric-cylinder case and a charge-conservation check. If the result changes by several percent as the mesh is refined, it should not be advertised as an accurate field prediction. Grid size and termination tolerance belong in a reproducible numerical result.\n\n## Sensitivity and design interpretation\n\nHalving mesh spacing increases the number of interior nodes by approximately four in a two-dimensional regular domain.\n\n\n## Why the model can fail\n\nIn a conductor approximation, imposing both potential and normal derivative on the same boundary generally overconstrains the model. Check global conservation: total outward displacement flux should match enclosed free charge.\n\n\n## Two resolved conceptual checkpoints\n\n**Question A.** If a numerical residual is tiny but peak E changes on mesh refinement, is the model validated?\n\n**Reasoned answer.** No. Iteration convergence is not spatial convergence; the grid may not resolve the local gradient or corner field.\n\n**Question B.** Why does differentiating V amplify discretization noise?\n\n**Reasoned answer.** The finite-difference derivative divides voltage differences by grid spacing, so small potential errors can create disproportionately large field errors.\n\n## Next steps\n\n[Unit 3 interactive model](../simulations/model-03.html) \u00b7 [Assessment](../assessments/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Project](../projects/unit-03.html) \u00b7 [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html).\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-3-6",
          "secNumber": "3.6",
          "heading": "Computational Simulation and Design Project: Gauss Law, Electric Flux Density, and Divergence",
          "content": "# Unit 3 engineering project: Potential solver for a capacitor cross-section\n\n## Problem statement and intended decision\n\nConstruct a charge-free 2D rectangular domain containing two electrode boundaries at 0 and 20 V. Start with a 1D parallel-plate benchmark and then add a central notch to one electrode. Use a five-point finite-difference stencil away from dielectric interfaces and enforce prescribed boundary nodes every iteration. Report max potential residual, peak field location, and a three-level mesh convergence comparison. The engineering goal is to locate candidate high-stress corners, not to claim a certified breakdown voltage from an unresolved geometric singularity.\n\n## Detailed investigation plan\n\nImplement a 2D five-point-stencil potential solver, starting with a region with fixed left and right electrode potentials and insulated top and bottom boundaries. Because top and bottom are Neumann boundaries, carefully mirror or difference the normal derivative so it vanishes; do not silently clamp them to zero volts. Compare with the 1D analytical linear ramp. Define a residual R_ij=4V_ij-(V_{i+1,j}+V_{i-1,j}+V_{i,j+1}+V_{i,j-1}). Show both the maximum residual and max deviation from the benchmark. Refining the grid should not change the exact ideal ramp beyond roundoff and incomplete iteration.\n\nIntroduce a dielectric interface with a different \u03b5r on one half. The stencil must change to preserve normal D continuity; using simple unweighted arithmetic averages across the interface is incorrect. Derive the face-flux balance using conductance-like coefficients epsilon_face/h\u00b2. Compare grid solutions with a hand-calculated one-dimensional layered slab. Plot normal E on both sides of the interface; its discontinuity is physical while Dn remains continuous without free interfacial charge. Report grid dimensions, dielectric assumptions, stopping tolerance and an energy estimate to enable reproducibility.\n\n## Submission requirements\n\nFollow the seven evidence deliverables and the 100-point project rubric in the [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html). The unit-specific source model, investigation plan, parameter choices and physical limitations above determine whether the report is adequate.\n\n## Submission safeguard\n\nAll activities are **simulation and analysis only**. If a project might ordinarily be performed with mains current, charged capacitors, electromagnets, high-voltage insulation or RF power, replace hardware with the ideal mathematical model and a synthetic dataset. Do not treat this project brief as authorization or guidance to perform dangerous laboratory measurements.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-3-7",
          "secNumber": "3.7",
          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Gauss Law, Electric Flux Density, and Divergence",
          "content": "# Unit 3 assessment \u2014 Electric potential and boundary-value problems\n\n**Instructions:** Work independently before expanding answers. These twelve questions cover all four lessons. State your coordinate reference, units, sign convention and physical assumptions. The answers below explain the expected reasoning; identical numerical output alone is insufficient.\n\n## Question 1 \u2014 Potential, work and path independence\n\n**Prompt:** How does E relate to the spacing of equipotential contours?\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary>\n\nWhere nearby equipotentials differ by the same voltage across a shorter distance, |E| is larger; E points perpendicular toward decreasing V. The matching lesson and shared study guide provide additional verification questions.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 2 \u2014 Potential, work and path independence\n\n**Prompt:** Does choosing a different ground reference alter force between charges?\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary>\n\nNo. Adding a constant to V leaves grad V unchanged, so E and electrostatic force are unaffected. The matching lesson and shared study guide provide additional verification questions.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 3 \u2014 Potential, work and path independence numerical and decision challenge\n\n**Prompt:** Independently derive and check this scenario: Across ideal plates 40 V apart by 4 mm, E magnitude is 10 kV/m. Moving +1 nC down the potential by 40 V releases field work 40 nJ. Then choose a parameter to change, predict its effect from doubling parallel-plate separation at fixed voltage halves E, whereas doubling voltage at fixed separation doubles E. State which quantities remain fixed and identify one important excluded physical effect.\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning and review criteria</summary>\n\n**Reference calculation:** Across ideal plates 40 V apart by 4 mm, E magnitude is 10 kV/m. Moving +1 nC down the potential by 40 V releases field work 40 nJ.\n\n**Sensitivity logic:** Doubling parallel-plate separation at fixed voltage halves E, whereas doubling voltage at fixed separation doubles E.\n\n**Engineering qualification:** A negative test charge moves toward higher potential if it follows the electrostatic force. Potential increases and decreases should not be confused with potential energy, which is U=qV and changes sign with q. The final report must include a quantity with correct SI units, an analytical limit and a specific reason the ideal calculation is not sufficient for hardware sign-off.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 4 \u2014 Poisson\u2019s and Laplace\u2019s equations\n\n**Prompt:** Why does no volume free charge produce Laplace rather than Poisson?\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary>\n\nWith rho_free=0 in a homogeneous linear dielectric, the source term of Poisson vanishes. Boundary voltages still generate nonzero V and E. The matching lesson and shared study guide provide additional verification questions.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 5 \u2014 Poisson\u2019s and Laplace\u2019s equations\n\n**Prompt:** What mistake results from applying an equal-spacing five-point stencil across sharply different \u03b5?\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary>\n\nIt ignores flux continuity; a conservative face-weighted discretization is required at material interfaces. The matching lesson and shared study guide provide additional verification questions.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 6 \u2014 Poisson\u2019s and Laplace\u2019s equations numerical and decision challenge\n\n**Prompt:** Independently derive and check this scenario: In a charge-free 1D region with V(0)=10 V and V(.02)=30 V, V(x)=10+1000x volts and E=-1000 V/m throughout. Then choose a parameter to change, predict its effect from doubling all lengths while preserving electrode voltages approximately halves E for geometrically similar charge-free Laplace solutions. State which quantities remain fixed and identify one important excluded physical effect.\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning and review criteria</summary>\n\n**Reference calculation:** In a charge-free 1D region with V(0)=10 V and V(.02)=30 V, V(x)=10+1000x volts and E=-1000 V/m throughout.\n\n**Sensitivity logic:** Doubling all lengths while preserving electrode voltages approximately halves E for geometrically similar charge-free Laplace solutions.\n\n**Engineering qualification:** Laplace\u2019s equation does not say potential is constant; linear and curved harmonic solutions exist. At an interface between unequal permittivities, one must apply boundary conditions rather than continue the same single-material equation blindly. The final report must include a quantity with correct SI units, an analytical limit and a specific reason the ideal calculation is not sufficient for hardware sign-off.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 7 \u2014 Uniqueness and boundary conditions\n\n**Prompt:** What extra constraint is required for a floating conductor?\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary>\n\nIts unknown constant potential must be solved together with the total charge condition. Setting it to ground changes the physical problem. The matching lesson and shared study guide provide additional verification questions.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 8 \u2014 Uniqueness and boundary conditions\n\n**Prompt:** What information does a pure-Neumann formulation fail to determine?\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary>\n\nAn arbitrary additive constant of potential remains; charge compatibility and a reference convention are needed. The matching lesson and shared study guide provide additional verification questions.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 9 \u2014 Uniqueness and boundary conditions numerical and decision challenge\n\n**Prompt:** Independently derive and check this scenario: If both electrodes shift from (0,20) V to (100,120) V, E remains unchanged. If only the high electrode shifts to 120 V, E changes fivefold relative to the original 20 V difference. Then choose a parameter to change, predict its effect from raising both prescribed electrode voltages by the same constant leaves E unchanged, but changing only one boundary changes the field. State which quantities remain fixed and identify one important excluded physical effect.\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning and review criteria</summary>\n\n**Reference calculation:** If both electrodes shift from (0,20) V to (100,120) V, E remains unchanged. If only the high electrode shifts to 120 V, E changes fivefold relative to the original 20 V difference.\n\n**Sensitivity logic:** Raising both prescribed electrode voltages by the same constant leaves E unchanged, but changing only one boundary changes the field.\n\n**Engineering qualification:** A floating conductor cannot be modeled automatically as a prescribed zero-voltage conductor; its net charge and unknown potential must be solved together. Numerical instability near corners may come from boundary idealization, not a programming bug. The final report must include a quantity with correct SI units, an analytical limit and a specific reason the ideal calculation is not sufficient for hardware sign-off.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 10 \u2014 Finite-difference electrostatics and mesh checks\n\n**Prompt:** If a numerical residual is tiny but peak E changes on mesh refinement, is the model validated?\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary>\n\nNo. Iteration convergence is not spatial convergence; the grid may not resolve the local gradient or corner field. The matching lesson and shared study guide provide additional verification questions.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 11 \u2014 Finite-difference electrostatics and mesh checks\n\n**Prompt:** Why does differentiating V amplify discretization noise?\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary>\n\nThe finite-difference derivative divides voltage differences by grid spacing, so small potential errors can create disproportionately large field errors. The matching lesson and shared study guide provide additional verification questions.\n\n</details>\n\n## Question 12 \u2014 Finite-difference electrostatics and mesh checks numerical and decision challenge\n\n**Prompt:** Independently derive and check this scenario: A 1D plate split into ten equal 1 mm cells with 100 V across it has node potentials 0,10,...,100 V. The residual of the constant-step second difference is zero in exact arithmetic. Then choose a parameter to change, predict its effect from halving mesh spacing increases the number of interior nodes by approximately four in a two-dimensional regular domain. State which quantities remain fixed and identify one important excluded physical effect.\n\n<details><summary>Reveal worked reasoning and review criteria</summary>\n\n**Reference calculation:** A 1D plate split into ten equal 1 mm cells with 100 V across it has node potentials 0,10,...,100 V. The residual of the constant-step second difference is zero in exact arithmetic.\n\n**Sensitivity logic:** Halving mesh spacing increases the number of interior nodes by approximately four in a two-dimensional regular domain.\n\n**Engineering qualification:** In a conductor approximation, imposing both potential and normal derivative on the same boundary generally overconstrains the model. Check global conservation: total outward displacement flux should match enclosed free charge. The final report must include a quantity with correct SI units, an analytical limit and a specific reason the ideal calculation is not sufficient for hardware sign-off.\n\n</details>\n\n"
        }
