          "id": "sec-5-6",
          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "Computational Simulation and Design Project: Conductors, Dielectrics, Boundary Conditions, and Capacitance",
          "content": "# Unit 5 engineering project: Nonuniform current and loss review\n\n## Problem statement and intended decision\n\nA resistive sensing film has uniform thickness but narrowing width. Build a one-dimensional width-dependent approximation that conserves total current and evaluate local J, E and Joule heating along its length. Compare integrated power with terminal VI. Then explain where the approximation fails near abrupt width transitions, requiring a two-dimensional conductivity solve. Use at least three widths and document source voltage, conductivity and thermal boundary assumptions. Finish with a measurement plan using four-wire voltage sensing to separate contact drop.\n\n## Detailed investigation plan\n\nTreat a uniform conductive bar as a resistor at first. Calculate R, I, J, E and dissipated power using terminal V and known \u03c3. Verify the local power integral matches VI to rounding precision. Now divide the same bar into two serial segments of equal length but different cross-sectional areas. Because steady current must be equal in both, the narrower segment has greater J and E. Integrate voltage drop along the length, rather than attempting to assign one uniform gradient to the full device. This introduces field-based resistance as a physical consequence of boundary and conservation laws.\n\nExtend the discussion to a rounded constriction in a two-dimensional conductive sheet. There is no simple exact one-dimensional current-density profile near the taper; show the limitations of a series-slab estimate. Explain why contacts contribute spreading resistance and how a four-terminal measurement can isolate the sensing region. The project is complete only when it states the ambient temperature used to select \u03c3 and identifies whether electrode contact resistance is included.\n\n## Submission requirements\n\nFollow the seven evidence deliverables and the 100-point project rubric in the [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html). The unit-specific source model, investigation plan, parameter choices and physical limitations above determine whether the report is adequate.\n\n## Submission safeguard\n\nAll activities are **simulation and analysis only**. If a project might ordinarily be performed with mains current, charged capacitors, electromagnets, high-voltage insulation or RF power, replace hardware with the ideal mathematical model and a synthetic dataset. Do not treat this project brief as authorization or guidance to perform dangerous laboratory measurements.\n"
