          "id": "sec-7-6",
          "secNumber": "7.6",
          "heading": "Computational Simulation and Design Project: Steady Electric Currents, Resistance, and Continuity",
          "content": "# Unit 7 engineering project: Solenoid and actuator trade-offs\n\n## Problem statement and intended decision\n\nDesign an idealized moving-armature coil with L(x) specified at five positions and constant drive current. Estimate force from numerical dL/dx, plot stored energy and show the effect of changing current by a factor of two. Then construct an air-gap-reluctance approximation and compare qualitative force direction. Flag which quantities require physical characterization: B\u2013H curve, friction, eddy-current loss, mechanical limits and coil heating. The result should distinguish an ideal electromechanical feasibility calculation from hardware validation.\n\n## Detailed investigation plan\n\nUse a set of five measured inductance values L(x) for a movable armature, construct a smooth graph and estimate central-difference dL/dx. At constant current I, calculate the force F=0.5I\u00b2dL/dx. Explain why derivative noise can make force estimates unstable even when individual L estimates appear precise; compare central difference with a least-squares local slope. Show the change in estimated force when the position sampling step is halved. Do not claim actuator force can be inferred from energy at a single mechanical position.\n\nAs a separate magnetic-circuit approximation, estimate flux through a core dominated by an air gap. Explicitly include ampere-turns, gap length, effective area and the assumption of a high-permeability unsaturated core. Calculate the field pressure B\u00b2/(2\u03bc0) as an order-of-magnitude estimate and compare force pressure times area with the energy-gradient estimate. A mismatch is an opportunity to examine fringe flux, nonlinearity and geometric modeling, not to tune a constant until two unrelated models agree.\n\n## Submission requirements\n\nFollow the seven evidence deliverables and the 100-point project rubric in the [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html). The unit-specific source model, investigation plan, parameter choices and physical limitations above determine whether the report is adequate.\n\n## Submission safeguard\n\nAll activities are **simulation and analysis only**. If a project might ordinarily be performed with mains current, charged capacitors, electromagnets, high-voltage insulation or RF power, replace hardware with the ideal mathematical model and a synthetic dataset. Do not treat this project brief as authorization or guidance to perform dangerous laboratory measurements.\n"
