          "id": "sec-8-6",
          "secNumber": "8.6",
          "heading": "Computational Simulation and Design Project: Magnetostatics: Biot-Savart Law, Amp\u00e8re Law, and Vector Potential",
          "content": "# Unit 8 engineering project: From capacitor transients to traveling waves\n\n## Problem statement and intended decision\n\nFirst model a 100 pF capacitor charged by a fixed current and plot the changing voltage and displacement current. Next model a 100-turn pickup loop in a time-varying magnetic field, reporting induced emf sign relative to a declared normal. Finally create a forward lossless plane wave with E and H amplitudes satisfying the intrinsic impedance. Use these three demonstrations to explain how Amp\u00e8re\u2013Maxwell, Faraday and energy transport fit together. A conceptual bridge is required; do not assert that the capacitor near field itself equals a uniform far-field wave.\n\n## Detailed investigation plan\n\nMake three links between circuit quantities and fields. First, charge an ideal parallel-plate capacitor with a constant terminal current. At fixed C, V(t) rises linearly, and the displacement flux derivative matches the wire conduction current for a spanning surface through its gap. Second, consider an isolated conducting loop whose magnetic flux varies linearly; calculate signed induced emf from the declared loop normal and contour orientation. Third, construct a uniform plane wave with E along x, H along y and propagation along +z. From the vacuum intrinsic impedance, compute H for a chosen E0 and then evaluate average Poynting power density.\n\nThe final synthesis should state what is, and is not, shared between the three systems. A capacitor near field can be approximately quasi-static; a wave is a distributed propagating solution. The induction loop can transfer energy between mechanical, magnetic and electrical systems, while the wave transports power through space. Maxwell equations unify the description but do not make these three geometries identical.\n\n## Submission requirements\n\nFollow the seven evidence deliverables and the 100-point project rubric in the [Shared study guidance](../STUDY-GUIDE.html). The unit-specific source model, investigation plan, parameter choices and physical limitations above determine whether the report is adequate.\n\n## Submission safeguard\n\nAll activities are **simulation and analysis only**. If a project might ordinarily be performed with mains current, charged capacitors, electromagnets, high-voltage insulation or RF power, replace hardware with the ideal mathematical model and a synthetic dataset. Do not treat this project brief as authorization or guidance to perform dangerous laboratory measurements.\n"
